course: dictionary
lesson: 14516
title: Noticing the very small things
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Perceptible is a formal word, and you can work out its meaning from the words around it. «The change was barely perceptible.» Barely tells you the change was very small, so perceptible must mean something you can only just notice.
explain: «There was a perceptible change in the temperature of the water.» A change you can notice, such as the water feeling a bit warmer, is perceptible. Ask yourself: could someone see, hear, feel or notice this? If yes, it is perceptible.
explain: Perceptible comes from the same family as perceive, which means to notice with your senses. Its opposite is imperceptible, meaning too small to notice.
fact: Perceptible means able to be seen, heard, felt or noticed.
fact: Perceptible can mean even if only just, so a perceptible change may be very small.
fact: «A barely perceptible sound» means a sound so quiet you can only just notice it.
fact: Scientists ask whether a change is perceptible to the senses, or whether it needs an instrument, such as a thermometer, to measure it.
fact: The opposite of perceptible is imperceptible, meaning too small to notice.
exercise: «The breeze was barely perceptible on my cheek.» What does perceptible mean here? | Hidden from view | Very strong and loud | Hot and sticky | *Just able to be felt
exercise: In a science test, Mia writes, «The change was perceptible.» What does she mean? | The change was wet | The change was too late | The change happened in the past | *The change could be noticed
exercise: Which sentence uses perceptible correctly? | Perceptible, Sam ran home quickly. | The perceptible clock was on the wall, far away. | The jump in temperature was perceptible on the thermometer. | *The jump in temperature was perceptible to Sam, who felt the warm air.
test: «The clock ticked, but the sound was barely perceptible.» What does barely perceptible mean? | Loud enough to wake the whole house | *Only just able to be heard | Lost in the wind | Painted bright red
test: Sam asks why a thermometer is needed when testing a warm room. What is the best answer? | To measure a change that the senses cannot notice | *To measure a change that the senses cannot notice, so it is not perceptible | To make the room warmer | To change the colour of the room
exam: In «a perceptible change in temperature», which word means the same as perceptible? | *Noticeable | Frozen | Distant | Sleepy
